# gemm (PolyBench/C)

General matrix multiply, `C = alpha*A*B + beta*C`.

## Benchmark source

```
* * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
* PolyBench/C 4.2.1 (beta)  *
* * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
```

Copyright (c) 2011-2016 The Ohio State University.

Contact (for the original code):

- Louis-Noel Pouchet <pouchet@cse.ohio-state.edu>
- Tomofumi Yuki <tomofumi.yuki@inria.fr>

## Build

```bash
make
```

`make` builds `gemm_pim.exe`, `gemm_pim_fast.exe`, `gemm_pim_int4.exe`, `gemm_baseline.exe`.

| Executable | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `gemm_baseline.exe` | CPU baseline |
| `gemm_pim.exe` | PUD-instrumented version; also executes every bbop on the CPU |
| `gemm_pim_fast.exe` | PUD-instrumented version without CPU execution of bbops (used by `scripts/run_workloads.sh`) |
| `gemm_pim_int4.exe` | PUD-instrumented version with 4-bit data (writes `bbop_statistics_int4.csv` and `transposition_int4.csv`) |

All versions use the paper-scale dataset (`-DPAPER_DATASET`). Other dataset sizes are defined in `gemm.h`.

## Run

```bash
./gemm_pim_fast.exe
```

The kernel takes no arguments.

## Output

The PUD versions write `bbop_statistics.csv`, `transposition.csv`, and
`bbop_resolution.csv` to the working directory. See `../README.md` for their
format.
